{"id":931,"status":"Fixed","summary":"arguments is moved to another scope","labels":["Type-Defect","Priority-Medium"],"stars":0,"commentCount":6,"comments":[{"id":0,"commenterId":-4866375781020184057,"content":"Using ADVANCED_OPTIMIZATIONS with CompilerOptions.collapsePropertiesOnExternTypes = true a script I used broke, it was something like:

function () {
  return function () {
    var arg = arguments;
    setTimeout(function() { alert(args); }, 0);
  }
}

Unfortunately it was rewritten to:

function () {
  return function () {
    setTimeout(function() { alert(arguments); }, 0);
  }
}

arguments should not be collapsed.","timestamp":1361893172,"attachments":[]},{"id":1,"commenterId":-5060162230523776870,"content":"Can you provide an exact snippet, I'm not able to reproduce this problem.  Also, note that code that you provide here has a typo ("arg" vs. "args").

CompilerOptions.collapsePropertiesOnExternTypes should also have nothing to do with inlining variables.
","timestamp":1361913805,"attachments":[]},{"id":2,"commenterId":-7699928860083865744,"content":"Yeah, we almost always model "arguments" as "a global variable that can change over time." If there was a bug where a local alias "arguments" was getting inlined, then it's likely that bug would affect all global variables, and some project would have run into it by now.

